WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.660
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:10.120
Deep Learning means that we are working with
neural networks that contain many inner layers.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:15.559
As neurons in each layer combine information
from the layer before, the deeper we go in

00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:18.940
these networks, the more elaborate details
we're going to see.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.620
Let's have a look at an example.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:26.000
For instance, if we train a neural network
to recognize images of human faces, first

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.509
we'll see an edge detector, and as a combination
of edges, object parts will emerge in the

00:00:31.509 --> 00:00:32.580
next layer.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:37.910
And in the later layers, a combination of
object parts create object models.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:43.430
Neural texture synthesis is about creating
lots of new images based on an input texture,

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:48.000
and these new images have to resemble, but
not copy the input.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.650
Previous works on neural texture synthesis
focused on how different features in a given

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:56.260
layer relate to the ones before and after
it.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:01.820
The issue is that because neurons in convolutional
neural networks are endowed with a small receptive

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:06.360
field, they can only look at an input texture
at one scale.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:11.070
So for instance, if you look here, you see
that with previous techniques, trying to create

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:16.370
small-scale details in a synthesized texture
is going to lead to rather poor results.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:21.500
This new method is about changing the inputs
and the outputs of the network to be able

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.660
to process these images at different scales.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:29.540
These scales range from coarser to finer versions
of the same images.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.620
Sound simple enough, right?

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:37.540
This simple idea makes all the difference
- here, you can see the input texture, and

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:38.990
here is the output.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:44.140
As you can see, it has different patterns
but has very similar properties to the input,

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:49.770
and if we zoom into both of these images,
we see that this one is able to create beautiful,

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:52.220
high-frequency details as well.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.950
Wow, this is some really, really crisp output.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:01.620
Now, it has to be emphasized that this means
that the statistical properties of the original

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.250
image are being mimiced really well.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:09.069
What it doesn't mean is that it takes into
consideration the meaning of these images.

00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:12.480
Just have a look at the synthesized bubbles
or the flowers here.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.110
The statistical properties of the synthesized
textures may be correct, but the semantic

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.910
meaning of the input is not captured well.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:24.290
In a future work, it would be super useful
to extend this algorithm to have a greater

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:29.430
understanding of the structure and the symmetries
of the input images into consideration.

